<h2 id="h-arrow-weight-for-deer-elk-and-bigger-game">Arrow Weight for Deer, Elk and Bigger Game</h2>
<p>Your game sets how much margin you want. A whitetail at 20 yards is a very different shot from a bull elk quartering away at 45 yards. Bigger animals have thicker hide, heavier bone and more muscle, so many hunters add weight for them.</p>
<?= data_table(
    ['Game', 'Common GPP range', 'Example at 60 lb', 'Example at 70 lb'],
    [
        ['Turkey, small game', '5&ndash;6', '300&ndash;360 gr', '350&ndash;420 gr'],
        ['Whitetail, pronghorn', '6&ndash;7', '360&ndash;420 gr', '420&ndash;490 gr'],
        ['Mule deer, black bear', '6.5&ndash;7.5', '390&ndash;450 gr', '455&ndash;525 gr'],
        ['Elk, moose, large bear', '7&ndash;8+', '420&ndash;480+ gr', '490&ndash;560+ gr'],
    ],
    'General arrow weight guidance by game (always tune your own setup)'
) ?>
<p><strong>Arrow weight for deer:</strong> a well-tuned arrow of about 6 to 7 GPP with a sharp broadhead is plenty for whitetail at normal bow ranges. Shot placement matters far more than an extra 30 grains.</p>
<p><strong>Arrow weight for elk:</strong> many elk hunters go to 7 GPP or more, often with a heavier broadhead or a weighted insert to raise FOC. Longer shots and big shoulder bones are the reason. If you hunt with a lighter draw weight, extra arrow weight helps you keep momentum up.</p>
<p>Shooting a lighter bow, like a youth bow or a setup around 50 pounds? Look at our <?= post_link('bear-archery-alaskan-xt-rth-compound-bow', 'Bear Alaskan XT RTH review') ?> for an example of an adjustable hunting bow, and size your arrow to the draw weight you actually shoot, not the bow&rsquo;s max.</p>

<h2 id="h-arrow-spine-chart-basics">Arrow Spine Chart Basics: Weight Isn&rsquo;t Everything</h2>
<p>Spine is how much an arrow shaft bends. It is listed as a number like 300, 340 or 400. That number is the deflection in thousandths of an inch under a standard test, so <strong>a lower number means a stiffer shaft</strong>. A 300-spine arrow is stiffer than a 400.</p>
<p>When you shoot, the arrow flexes as it leaves the bow. If the spine is wrong, the arrow fishtails or porpoises, and broadheads exaggerate the problem. Three things decide what spine you need:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Draw weight:</strong> more pounds need a stiffer (lower number) shaft.</li>
  <li><strong>Arrow length:</strong> a longer arrow acts weaker, so it may need a stiffer shaft.</li>
  <li><strong>Point weight:</strong> a heavier point or broadhead makes the arrow act weaker.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every arrow maker publishes its own spine chart, and charts differ between brands and models. Look up your draw weight, cut arrow length and point weight on the chart for the exact shaft you plan to buy. If you land between two sizes, many shooters pick the stiffer one when they plan to use heavy heads.</p>
<?= callout('tip', 'Change one thing, re-check spine', 'Moving from a 100-grain to a 125-grain broadhead, or adding a heavy insert, can push you into the next spine size. Check the chart again whenever you change point weight or arrow length.') ?>

<h2 id="h-what-is-foc">What Is FOC and How Much Do You Need?</h2>
<p>FOC means <strong>front-of-center</strong>. It describes how far the balance point sits ahead of the arrow&rsquo;s middle, as a percentage of its length. A higher FOC means more weight up front.</p>
<p>Here is a simple way to measure it:</p>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Measure arrow length</strong> (L) from the bottom of the nock groove to the end of the shaft.</li>
  <li><strong>Find the balance point</strong> with the point or broadhead on. Balance it on a knife edge and measure from the nock groove to that spot (B).</li>
  <li><strong>Calculate:</strong> FOC % = (B &minus; L/2) &divide; L &times; 100.</li>
</ol>
<p>Example: a 28-inch arrow that balances 17.5 inches from the nock groove has (17.5 &minus; 14) &divide; 28 &times; 100 = 12.5% FOC.</p>
<p>Many target and hunting arrows fall around 10 to 15% FOC, and some big-game hunters build arrows well above that. More FOC tends to help broadheads steer and fly straight, and can help penetration. The trade-off is that adding point weight weakens dynamic spine and adds total weight, which slows the arrow down.</p>
<p>Common ways to raise FOC include a heavier broadhead, a brass or steel insert, a weight collar, or lighter fletching and nocks at the back.</p>

<h2 id="h-heavy-vs-light-arrows">Heavy vs. Light Arrows: Kinetic Energy and Momentum</h2>
<p>This is the core of the debate. A lighter arrow leaves the bow faster. A heavier arrow leaves slower but hits harder in a different way. Two formulas show the difference, and they are the same ones our calculators use:</p>
<?= spec_table([
    ['Kinetic energy (ft-lb)', 'arrow weight (gr) &times; speed (fps)&sup2; &divide; 450,240'],
    ['Momentum (slug-ft/s)', 'arrow weight (gr) &times; speed (fps) &divide; 225,218'],
], 'Arrow energy formulas') ?>
<p>Kinetic energy squares the speed, so it rewards fast arrows. Momentum grows evenly with weight, so it rewards heavy arrows. Momentum is often seen as a better hint of how well an arrow keeps pushing through hide and bone after impact.</p>
<p>Here is how three arrows might compare out of the same bow. The speeds are made-up examples for illustration, not test results; your bow will differ.</p>
<?= data_table(
    ['Arrow', 'Example speed', 'Kinetic energy', 'Momentum'],
    [
        ['350 gr (light)', '300 fps', '70.0 ft-lb', '0.466'],
        ['425 gr (middle)', '280 fps', '74.0 ft-lb', '0.528'],
        ['500 gr (heavy)', '260 fps', '75.1 ft-lb', '0.577'],
    ],
    'Illustrative comparison: same bow, different arrow weights'
) ?>
<p>Notice the pattern. Kinetic energy barely changes, but momentum climbs about 24% from the lightest to the heaviest arrow. A heavier arrow also tends to absorb more of the bow&rsquo;s energy, so the shot is usually quieter with less hand shock.</p>
<p>So why shoot light arrows at all? Speed flattens trajectory. If you misjudge range by 5 yards, a fast arrow drops less, which can mean the difference between a hit and a miss. Fast arrows also reach the animal sooner, giving it less time to react to the shot.</p>
<?= tool_cta('kinetic-energy-calculator') ?>
<h3 id="h-the-practical-middle-ground">The Practical Middle Ground</h3>
<p>At Better Outdoors we recommend most whitetail hunters start around the middle of the range, roughly 6.5 GPP, and then build FOC with the point and insert. You keep a fairly flat trajectory, plenty of momentum for deer, and good broadhead flight. Go heavier if you hunt elk or bear, take steep angles, or shoot lower draw weights.</p>

<h2 id="h-how-to-choose-your-arrow-weight-step-by-step">How to Choose Your Arrow Weight, Step by Step</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Weigh your bow.</strong> Use a bow scale to find your real peak draw weight. Many bows read a few pounds off the label.</li>
  <li><strong>Pick a GPP target</strong> for your game: about 6&ndash;7 for deer, 7&ndash;8+ for elk and big game. Never under 5.</li>
  <li><strong>Find your spine</strong> on the arrow maker&rsquo;s chart using draw weight, cut length and point weight.</li>
  <li><strong>Add up the weight.</strong> Shaft grains per inch &times; length, plus insert, point, nock, vanes and wrap. A <?= amz('digital grain scale archery', 'digital grain scale') ?> makes this easy.</li>
  <li><strong>Check FOC</strong> and adjust with point or insert weight if needed. Re-check spine after any change.</li>
  <li><strong>Shoot and tune.</strong> Paper tune or bare-shaft tune, then confirm broadheads hit with field points.</li>
</ol>
