<h2 id="h-common-arrow-weight-mistakes">Common Arrow Weight Mistakes</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Going under 5 GPP</strong> to chase a speed number. It risks bow damage and rarely helps in the field.</li>
  <li><strong>Ignoring spine</strong> when changing points. A heavier head on the same shaft can wreck broadhead flight.</li>
  <li><strong>Guessing total weight.</strong> Vanes, wraps and lighted nocks add up. Weigh a finished arrow.</li>
  <li><strong>Mixing arrows.</strong> Arrows in your quiver should match in weight within a few grains for consistent groups.</li>
  <li><strong>Skipping re-sighting.</strong> Any change in arrow weight changes speed and drop, so your pins move. Our guide on <?= post_link('how-to-sight-in-a-compound-bow', 'how to sight in a compound bow') ?> covers the full process.</li>
</ul>
<p>Shopping for complete arrows? Browse <?= amz('carbon hunting arrows 300 spine', 'carbon hunting arrows') ?> and <?= amz('fixed blade broadheads 125 grain', '125-grain broadheads') ?> once you know your spine and target weight.</p>

<h2 id="h-final-thoughts">Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>So, how heavy should your arrow be? Stay above 5 grains per pound, aim for roughly 6 to 7 GPP for deer and 7 to 8+ for elk and bigger game, match the spine to your setup, and build sensible FOC. Then shoot it. A tuned arrow you trust, with a sharp broadhead and good shot placement, beats any number on a chart.</p>

